# Line endings, and why this file exists.
#
# The repo had none, and `core.autocrlf=true` on the Windows dev machine means every text file is
# checked out with CRLF. That is invisible almost everywhere -- until Terraform reads a `.tf` file
# and compares it against live AWS, which holds what was applied from a LF checkout. The plan then
# proposes rewriting a CloudFront function and an alarm description whose only difference is \n
# versus \r\n: a real diff, semantically identical, on a function that runs on every viewer request.
#
# Two changes like that shipped in the v1.0.49 plan before anyone looked closely, and the standing
# rule for this repo is to read the rendered diff rather than the action counts. This is the fix for
# the class rather than the instance -- normalize to LF in the repo, so what Terraform renders is
# what was applied.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: store LF in git, check out LF, regardless of the platform's autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched. A "normalized" image or archive is a corrupted one.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.jar  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.p12  binary
*.jks  binary

# Windows scripts are the deliberate exception: cmd.exe is the one interpreter here that genuinely
# wants CRLF, and a .bat with LF line endings can fail in ways that read as a broken script rather
# than a broken checkout.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
